turnkey-mariadb-19.0 (9) turnkey; urgency=high

  * The build checks the mask of mariadb.socket and mariadb-extra.socket
    with the link that systemctl mask writes (/etc/systemd/system/<unit>
    to /dev/null). The systemctl of the build chroot has no is-enabled,
    so the layer build of 19.0-8 stopped there (step 12, 2026-10-10).
    19.0-8 was not released; this release has its content and this fix.

 -- Marcos Mendez <mendez.foto@gmail.com>  Sat, 10 Oct 2026 17:24:15 +0000

turnkey-mariadb-19.0 (8) turnkey; urgency=high

  * Security: 3306 is no longer on the uplink (#29). Debian's
    mariadb.socket has ListenStream=3306, which systemd binds as
    [::]:3306. pct create of Proxmox VE removes /etc/machine-id, so the
    first start of a container is the first boot of systemd, which enables
    every unit that no preset disables. On the nodes of the pair test
    (keel 0.23.3, 2026-10-08, finding B3) the socket held 3306 on every
    address, the server got its listeners from systemd, bind-address had
    no effect, and 3306 answered on the public uplink. The manifest says
    expose: mesh, never on the uplink.
  * conf.d/main disables and masks mariadb.socket and mariadb-extra.socket
    in the image, and stops the build when a socket unit of the tree can
    listen on a wildcard address for 3306 and is not masked
    (bin/keel-socket-wildcard, copied into the tree like
    bin/keel-project-packages and removed with it).
  * keel-mariadb 0.1.1 ships a preset that disables both units at a first
    boot, and its postinst masks both, for a machine whose first boot
    already ran. It does not restart the server.
  * The boot test makes each node as pct create does (no machine id, the
    00-pve.preset) and, after the first boot, checks that nothing listens
    on a wildcard address for 3306 and that the node address does not
    answer on 3306 from the host. The appliance job moves to packages.yml
    and installs the keel-mariadb package built from the branch before the
    first boot; the check keeps its name.

 -- Marcos Mendez <mendez.foto@gmail.com>  Fri, 09 Oct 2026 01:00:00 +0000

turnkey-mariadb-19.0 (7) turnkey; urgency=medium

  * The image ships the appliance manifest of Keel MariaDB,
    /usr/share/keel/appliances/mariadb.yaml, from the new package
    keel-mariadb (packages/keel-mariadb, handbook decision 0041), installed
    by plan/main from the Keel archive as keel-core and keel-web are. On
    19.0-5 only core.yaml existed, so a description saying appliance.name:
    mariadb was refused ("not an installed appliance under
    /usr/share/keel") and keel inspect inferred core (#25). The manifest:
    base core, whose five overlays (installer, wireguard, etcd, crowdsec,
    vip) it inherits with their defaults and adds none; mariadb.service on
    3306 as a mesh port with Monit's mysql check; the db_password secret,
    generated if asked and shared by a pair; the db_user option, default
    admin. A description can now declare the pair of docs/replication.md,
    appliance.vip and database.server.
  * conf.d/main proves keel-mariadb installed at the archive's candidate
    with the other project packages, and runs keel manifest validate
    mariadb on the built tree, so a manifest the booted machine would refuse
    fails the build. keel/instance.example.yaml names the appliance, the
    mode and the five overlays of a simple installation.
  * tests/package.bats builds and reads the package back, in the new check
    "packages / build"; tests/coverage.sh leaves it out of the kcov run.

 -- Marcos Mendez <mendez.foto@gmail.com>  Thu, 08 Oct 2026 21:00:00 +0000

turnkey-mariadb-19.0 (6) turnkey; urgency=medium

  * firstboot.d/35mysqlpass follows inithooks' rule for a first boot nobody
    can answer (lib/console.sh, INITHOOKS_UNATTENDED, the one 30rootpass and
    31fqdn follow) instead of testing its own standard input for a terminal.
    tty1 of an LXC container is a terminal nobody is attached to, so a
    headless first boot without DB_PASS drew the password box there and
    waited for good, re-asking when the dialog was killed (#24, two
    containers of 19.0-5 held for over twelve minutes). When nobody can
    answer and nothing is declared the hook asks nothing, says so in one
    line in the inithooks log and the journal, naming the field to declare,
    and finishes; the account stays as the build published it, unable to
    authenticate, until the description declares secrets.db_password or
    keel-init asks it at a console somebody is attached to. When somebody
    can answer, the dialog is used whatever the hook's standard input is.
  * conf.d/main fails the build on an inithooks without lib/console.sh
    (2.3.6+keel22 and later), which would leave the hook unable to run.
    tests/hook.bats runs the hook with a stand-in for that library.

 -- Marcos Mendez <mendez.foto@gmail.com>  Thu, 08 Oct 2026 20:30:00 +0000

turnkey-mariadb-19.0 (5) turnkey; urgency=medium

  * The project's packages (inithooks, confconsole, keel) come from the
    Keel archive, archive.keellinux.org, like every other Keel package,
    through the source and the 990 pin the parent layer carries
    (Keel-Linux/common#30; trixie-testing as well with KEEL_APT_TRACK=
    testing, common#32). The build host's staging archive is gone from the
    recipe: the Makefile no longer copies it into the bootstrap, and
    conf.d/main no longer pins it. It held inithooks 2.3.6+keel5,
    confconsole 2.2.3+keel2 and keel 0.3.5, and its pin at 1001 would have
    downgraded the layer to them. bin/keel-archive-check, which verified
    that copy, goes with it.
  * conf.d/main upgrades the three from the archive and then proves each is
    installed at apt's candidate, from the Keel archive, in the suite of the
    track (bin/keel-project-packages, copied into the tree for the build
    only), in place of the version floors.
  * The overlay no longer ships /etc/apt/sources.list.d/keel.sources
    (apt.keellinux.org, disabled) or /etc/apt/preferences.d/keel (the Keel
    origin at 1001). At 1001 apt downgraded every package newer than the
    archive's (tracker#23), and both files, at the paths common uses, would
    override the source and the 990 pin common ships. The build fails if any
    apt file of the image still names the staging archive.
    tests/apt-files.bats and tests/project-packages.bats check the recipe.

 -- Marcos Mendez <mendez.foto@gmail.com>  Fri, 02 Oct 2026 17:00:00 +0000

turnkey-mariadb-19.0 (4) turnkey; urgency=medium

  * bin/dbpass.py draws its password box on the terminal. firstboot.d/
    35mysqlpass reads the script's standard output for DB_PASS=, and dialog
    draws on standard output, so an interactive first boot froze at the
    MariaDB password with the box drawn into the hook's pipe (keel-wordpress
    19.0-3 on Proxmox, 2026-09-30). The answers now go to a copy of that
    pipe and standard output is the terminal. tests/dbpass.bats runs the
    script as the hook does, inside a pseudo terminal.

 -- Keel Linux maintainers <admin@keellinux.org>  Wed, 30 Sep 2026 04:15:40 +0000

turnkey-mariadb-19.0 (3) turnkey; urgency=low

  * bin/keel-archive-check refuses a trusted=yes on the project archive rather
    than on every apt source in the build tree. The captured pool of decision
    0012 sets Trusted: yes on purpose, for a file: index generated on this
    machine from files keel-pool verify checks against the same digests apt
    does, so the wider rule would have failed every pinned build. What the pool
    does is the pool's business; the project archive is verified (tracker#7).

 -- Keel Linux maintainers <admin@keellinux.org>  Sun, 27 Sep 2026 20:10:00 +0000

turnkey-mariadb-19.0 (2) turnkey; urgency=low

  * The build verifies the project's own APT archive instead of reading it
    unverified. The staging distribution has been signed since 2026-09-27, but
    the build environment had no copy of the public key and the source entry
    said [trusted=yes], which switches verification off: apt printed
    "W: OpenPGP signature verification failed ... Missing key
    8CFD1A4841448B2227341CEB202CACBD0E97090A" and installed inithooks,
    confconsole and keel anyway. The public half of the staging key is now
    installed into the build tree as
    /etc/apt/keyrings/keel-staging-keyring.asc, the source entry names it
    through signed-by, and apt-get update runs with --error-on=any, so a
    signature that cannot be checked fails the build instead of warning
    (tracker#7).

  * bin/keel-archive-check does the same checks itself rather than trusting
    apt to have complained: the copied package index is the live one, the
    source entry names the keyring and this distribution, no apt source in
    the tree says trusted=yes, and the signature on the copied InRelease
    verifies against the named key with gpgv. It runs where the copy is made
    and again on the tree that is about to be configured, which is a step
    where no apt-get update runs at all.

  * conf.d/main removes the keyring with the source entry and the copy of the
    archive: the staging key signs whatever the build host produced, so an
    image that kept it would carry trust in a nightly. The removelist
    common/removelists-final/turnkey takes all three out as well, whatever a
    recipe does.

  * The floor for keel rises from 0.2.1 to 0.3.5. The two node gate configures
    replication from the instance description on the booted machine, which
    needs the database section, the role configuration apply writes and
    promotion; a layer carrying keel 0.2.1 answers "database: unknown top
    level key" and the gate fails, as it did on keel-mariadb#10.

 -- Keel Linux maintainers <admin@keellinux.org>  Sun, 27 Sep 2026 19:30:00 +0000

turnkey-mariadb-19.0 (1) turnkey; urgency=low

  * Initial release of the MariaDB database layer for Keel, compatible with
    TurnKey Linux appliances. Built as a layer on core:
    bt-layer mariadb --parent core.

  * MariaDB 11.8 from Debian 13 (Trixie), listening on ::1 and 127.0.0.1
    only (overlay/etc/mysql/mariadb.conf.d/99-keel-bind.cnf).

  * webmin-mysql, so the database is administered from the Webmin panel
    core already carries, at https://[address]:12321.

  * An administrative account, 'admin', created unusable at build time and
    given its password at first boot from the instance description:
    secrets.db_password renders to DB_PASS, which
    firstboot.d/35mysqlpass reads. This layer has no Adminer, so it ships
    the hook the Adminer overlay of common would otherwise have brought.

  * Adminer, lighttpd and php-fpm, which the upstream mysql appliance
    bundles, are deliberately left out: they belong to the web stack that
    LAMP builds on this layer.

 -- Keel Linux maintainers <admin@keellinux.org>  Sat, 27 Sep 2026 12:00:00 +0000
